### 4.4 Calibrated Reachability Guarantees via Conformal Prediction (CP)

The SLS formulation in Sec. [4.2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.14188v1#S4.SS2 "4.2 Simulator-Informed GPU-Parallel Output Feedback SLS ‣ 4 Method ‣ Robustness without Wrinkles: Parallel Simulation and Robust MPC for Certified Deformable Manipulation") requires uncertainty sets for the closed-loop dynamics and observation model to certify safety for the real system ([1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.14188v1#S3.E1 "In 3 Problem Statement ‣ Robustness without Wrinkles: Parallel Simulation and Robust MPC for Certified Deformable Manipulation")). On hardware, however, independent labels for the true process disturbance or state-estimation error are typically unavailable. Instead, we measure the discrepancy between the model-predicted next observation and the next observation returned by perception, and use CP to calibrate an aggregate one-step prediction error in the controller coordinates. In our implementation (Sec. [5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.14188v1#S5 "5 Results ‣ Robustness without Wrinkles: Parallel Simulation and Robust MPC for Certified Deformable Manipulation")), the perception estimates the full rope state from RGB-D images. Thus, in ([1b](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.14188v1#S3.E1.2 "In 1 ‣ 3 Problem Statement ‣ Robustness without Wrinkles: Parallel Simulation and Robust MPC for Certified Deformable Manipulation")), we take h⁡(q)=qh(q)=q, so yk+1=qk+eky\_{k+1}=q\_{k}+e\_{k} with ‖ek‖2≤ρ\|e\_{k}\|\_{2}\leq\rho, where ρ\rho is a worst-case measurement-error bound from camera accuracy specifications. We define the perceived state q¯k:=yk+1\bar{q}\_{k}:=y\_{k+1} as the noisy full-state estimate used by the controller. Although the physical system evolves on latent qkq\_{k}, our SLS implementation plans over q¯k\bar{q}\_{k}; the CP-calibrated residual certifies tubes for q¯k\bar{q}\_{k}, which are conservatively inflated by ρ\rho to obtain true-state tubes.

